  "prediction": "The Small Business Administration's (SBA) 7(a) loan program provides guarantees to commercial lenders for loans made to small businesses for working capital and other general business purposes. The program aims to increase access to capital for small businesses that cannot obtain credit at reasonable terms. However, a review of the program found that lenders have a high rate of noncompliance with the credit elsewhere requirement, which is a critical component of the 7(a) program.\n\nThe credit elsewhere requirement ensures that lenders verify that borrowers have exhausted all other credit options before receiving an SBA-guaranteed loan. However, the review found that lenders often fail to document their credit elsewhere decisions, and SBA's oversight of lenders is limited. The review also found that SBA does not collect or analyze sufficient data on lenders' use of the credit elsewhere criteria, making it difficult to identify patterns in lender practices.\n\nThe review analyzed data from fiscal years 2007 to 2016 and found that the majority of 7(a) loans went to new businesses, women-owned businesses, and businesses located in distressed areas. However, the review also found that lenders with larger numbers of SBA loans tended to have more creditworthy borrowers.\n\nThe review made three recommendations to SBA to improve its oversight of lenders and ensure compliance with the credit elsewhere requirement. The recommendations include:\n\n1. Requiring reviewers to consistently document their assessments of a lender's policies and practices.\n2. Using on-site and off-site reviews to routinely collect information on lenders' use of the credit elsewhere criteria.\n3. Analyzing information on lenders' use of the credit elsewhere criteria to identify lenders that may be at risk of noncompliance.\n\nOverall, the review found that SBA's 7(a) loan program is critical to increasing access to capital for small businesses, but that lenders' noncompliance with the credit elsewhere requirement is a significant issue that needs to be addressed.",
